  (:adr/body "# ADR 2799007532: cloud-itonami ISCO-08 7532 Garment and Related Patternmakers and Cutters cutting-workshop scheduling/logistics coordination coverage

## Status

Accepted

## Context

This is part of the occupation-classification track (sibling to the
`cloud-itonami-isic-*` industry-actor rollout, same governing
ADR-2607121000): codify every ISCO-08 occupation unit group as an
autonomous actor (LLM/advisor behind an independent Governor, a
`langgraph.graph` StateGraph, append-only audit ledger), modeled on a
robotics premise -- a robot performs the occupation's physical/
administrative work, proposing actions to an independent Governor
that gates them, never dispatches hardware itself, always escalates
high-stakes decisions to a human. This ADR covers **ISCO-08 class
7532 -- Garment and Related Patternmakers and Cutters** only, Wave3
(production/trades) of the rollout (Wave0/1/2 100% complete; per the
live git-trees listing immediately before this ADR, 65 sibling
`2799xxxxxx`-prefixed coverage ADRs had already landed on
`origin/main`, with additional sibling agents landing concurrently in
this same batch, so exact totals move faster than any single snapshot).

The `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's live `{:id 7532 ...}` entry
was verified against a fresh clone before any work began (`clojure -M
-e \"(require 'kotoba.occupation) (println
(kotoba.occupation/get-occupation \\\"7532\\\"))\"` from a checkout with
the `kotoba-lang/technology` sibling present at the correct
`../technology` relative path), and its `:name` read exactly Garment
and Related Patternmakers and Cutters -- confirming the premise, no
mismatch. Prior to this ADR the entry's `:maturity` was `:spec` with
`:required-technologies [:robotics :identity :audit-ledger]` and no
`:repo`/`:business-id`; no repository existed at
`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7532` (`gh api` 404 confirmed before
scaffolding). This ADR and its companion registry edit promote the
`7532` entry to `:implemented`.

Garment and related patternmakers and cutters operate rotary cutters,
cutting machines and shears on fabric -- a standard workshop
cutting-tool hazard. The controlling constraint for this vertical is
that the closed op allowlist must NEVER include any op that directly
finalizes a pattern-cutting-execution decision or a
workshop-safety-clearance decision, nor override a shop safety
officer's judgment -- those decisions must always be a hard permanent
block, never auto-commit-eligible. This actor is deliberately scoped
to WORKSHOP SCHEDULING/LOGISTICS COORDINATION ONLY -- it never
performs cutting work or makes workshop-safety-clearance decisions
itself.

The reference implementation mirrored for module SHAPE and the
closed-allowlist + independently-verified-provenance safety pattern is
[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7319`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7319)
(Handicraft Workers Not Elsewhere Classified: `store`/`advisor`/
`governor`/`actor` split, `langgraph.graph` StateGraph, full doc set,
same wave, a closely-related generic workshop scheduling/logistics
coordination pattern) -- cloned and read in full in this session. The
originally-nominated closer-domain reference,
`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7531` (Tailors, Dressmakers,
Furriers and Hatters), did not yet exist on GitHub at the time this
work began (confirmed via `gh api` 404), so the fallback reference was
used per the task's own contingency instruction.

## Decision

Publish `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7532` as an OSS workshop
scheduling/logistics-coordination actor for garment patternmakers and
cutters, following the SAME governed-actor architecture as every
prior `cloud-itonami-isco-*` actor in this fleet: **Cut Coordination
Advisor ⊣ CutCoordGovernor** on a `langgraph.graph` StateGraph
(`io.github.kotoba-lang/langgraph`, git-sha-pinned identically to the
reference's `deps.edn` -- a different library coordinate from the
`langgraph-clj` used by the unrelated `20-actors/*` actor family),
with an independent Governor, human-in-the-loop approval via
`interrupt-before`, and an append-only audit ledger.

This actor is deliberately **workshop scheduling/logistics
coordination ONLY, never direct pattern-cutting-execution or
workshop-safety-clearance authority** -- it must NEVER be designed to
exercise, simulate exercising, or propose exercising a
pattern-cutting-execution decision, a workshop-safety-clearance
decision, or any override of a shop safety officer's judgment. These
are not merely gated behind escalation -- they are structurally
absent from the op-allowlist entirely.

### Closed proposal-op allowlist (all `:effect :propose`)

- `:log-work-record` -- job/pattern/progress data logging
- `:schedule-crew-operation` -- crew/task scheduling proposal
- `:flag-safety-concern` -- surface a cutting-tool-hazard/
  equipment-condition concern, ALWAYS escalates, NEVER auto-commit
  eligible
- `:coordinate-supply-order` -- fabric/pattern-materials procurement
  proposal

This actor has **NO op, anywhere in its allowlist, that resembles** a
pattern-cutting-execution decision, a workshop-safety-clearance
decision, or a shop-safety-officer-judgment override. These are
**structurally absent from the op-allowlist entirely**, not merely
gated behind escalation -- an op such as
`:finalize-pattern-cutting-execution-decision` or
`:declare-workshop-safety-clearance` cannot even be proposed by the
reference mock advisor (its `infer`/`rationale-for` functions only
ever emit one of the four allowlist ops), and if any code path (a
forged proposal, a future LLM advisor, a misbehaving fork) ever
produced such an op, the governor's closed-allowlist HARD check
(`:unknown-op`) rejects it unconditionally and permanently. A second,
independent HARD check (`:scope-excluded-action`) names six
concretely-forbidden ops (`cutcoord.governor/scope-excluded-ops`:
`:finalize-pattern-cutting-execution-decision`,
`:finalize-cutting-operation`, `:declare-workshop-safety-clearance`,
`:declare-safety-clearance`, `:override-shop-safety-officer-judgment`,
`:override-safety-officer-judgment`) as defense-in-depth, and is
verified by dedicated unit tests
(`cutcoord.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-op-finalize-pattern-cutting-execution-decision`,
`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-finalize-cutting-operation`,
`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-declare-workshop-safety-clearance`,
`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-declare-safety-clearance`,
`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-override-shop-safety-officer-judgment`),
plus actor-level integration coverage
(`cutcoord.actor-test/holds-a-scope-excluded-op-even-at-high-confidence`,
`.../holds-a-workshop-safety-clearance-op-even-at-high-confidence`,
`.../holds-a-safety-officer-override-op-even-at-high-confidence`).

### Four HARD governor checks (permanent, un-overridable, plus the closed-allowlist check)

1. **Cutter unregistered** -- any proposal must resolve to an
   independently registered cutter (patternmaker/cutter) record
   (`:no-cutter`). Never trusts the proposal's own claim of who the
   cutter is.
2. **Workshop unregistered** -- any proposal must cite an
   independently registered garment cutting workshop site
   (`:no-workshop`). Together with check 1, this directly implements
   the CRITICAL invariant that a worker/workshop record must be
   independently verified/registered before any action.
3. **Effect not `:propose`** -- any other `:effect` value is a HARD
   block (a claim to directly actuate outside governance)
   (`:no-actuation`).
4. **Op not allowed / scope-excluded action** -- any `:op` outside the
   closed four-op allowlist is a HARD, PERMANENT block (`:unknown-op`),
   and independently, any proposal whose `:op` names one of the six
   concretely-forbidden ops above, OR whose free-text `:rationale`
   names a finalization/execution action for finalizing a
   pattern-cutting-execution decision, declaring the workshop
   safety-cleared, or overriding a shop safety officer's judgment, is
   a second HARD, PERMANENT block (`:scope-excluded-action`),
   evaluated unconditionally on content -- defense-in-depth on top of
   the allowlist check: even if a future change somehow widened the
   allowlist, content-based rejection still independently blocks
   pattern-cutting-execution/workshop-safety-clearance/safety-override
   language wherever it appears in free text.

### One always-escalate (SOFT) gate, one threshold gate, plus the general confidence floor

- `:flag-safety-concern` -- ALWAYS escalates to a human, regardless of
  confidence (governor's implicit always-risky check). This is the
  ONLY channel by which the robot may surface a cutting-tool-hazard/
  material-handling/equipment-condition observation -- it never
  resolves the concern itself, and the robot's role ends at here is
  the hazard data, never here is what I recommend doing about shop
  safety or workshop clearance.
- a `:coordinate-supply-order` proposal whose `:cost` exceeds
  `supply-cost-threshold` (2000, inclusive boundary verified by
  `ok-supply-order-at-threshold-boundary`) -- always needs human
  sign-off, independent of confidence. This is an ESCALATION, not a
  hard block, per the task's explicit domain design: an
  over-threshold fabric/pattern-materials order is not itself unsafe,
  unlike a pattern-cutting-execution or workshop-safety-clearance
  attempt, which is why it is handled by a different mechanism (soft
  escalate vs. structural absence).
- (advisor confidence below `confidence-floor` 0.6 also escalates, for
  any op.)

### Self-trip guard (fleet-known bug class, carried over from the sibling ISIC/ISCO tracks)

A governor's scope-exclusion term list phrased as a bare noun (e.g.
cutter, pattern, fabric, cutting) can accidentally match inside the
mock advisor's own default rationale text for a legitimate proposal,
self-blocking the actor's own happy path -- this is exactly the risk
this vertical's own vocabulary (a workshop coordination actor working
adjacent to garment cutting necessarily discusses those concepts to
do its scheduling/logging job) could trigger if phrased carelessly.
`cutcoord.governor/scope-excluded-phrases` is phrased exclusively as
finalization/execution ACTIONS (e.g. finalize the pattern-cutting
execution decision, finalize the cutting operation, declare the
workshop safety cleared, override the shop safety officer's judgment
-- matching the task's own worked example phrasing), never bare
nouns, and
`cutcoord.governor-test/default-mock-advisor-proposals-never-self-trip-on-scope-exclusion`
is a dedicated regression test asserting every op in the closed
allowlist, driven through the default mock advisor, never trips
`:scope-excluded-action`. The mock advisor's own default rationale
template was deliberately written to contain the bare nouns (logged
work record for cutter ..., scheduled crew operation for garment
cutting task ..., ...routed for shop safety officer review) WITHOUT
containing any of the full finalization-action phrases, so the
regression test is a genuine stress test of the bare-noun-vs-action-
phrase distinction, not a vacuous pass. Green on first run in this
session -- the scope-exclusion self-trip guard itself was never
tripped during development.
`cutcoord.actor-test/holds-a-scope-excluded-op-even-at-high-confidence`
and its workshop-safety-clearance/safety-officer-override siblings
additionally exercise the same invariant through the full graph run,
confirming a scope-excluded proposal never reaches `commit-record!`
regardless of disposition path.

## Consequences

- `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's `{:id 7532 ...}` entry promoted
  from `:spec` to `:implemented`, with `:repo`/`:business-id` set to
  the real repository
  (`https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7532` /
  `cloud-itonami-isco-7532`), and `maturity-summary`'s implemented
  count bumped accordingly (recomputed live from the registry via
  `kotoba.occupation/maturity-summary`, not assumed, immediately
  before the edit).
- Full test suite (`clojure -M:test`): 25 tests, 55 assertions, 0
  failures, 0 errors -- raw output captured in this ADR's
  `:test/counts` and in the task report, independently re-verified
  against a brand-new fresh clone of the pushed repository (not just
  the local working copy).
- No `manifest/west.yml` changes -- this actor is registered purely via
  the `kotoba-lang/occupation` capability registry, consistent with
  sibling `cloud-itonami-isco-*`/`cloud-itonami-isic-*` actors.
- No `kotoba-lang/technology` dependency was added to the actor's
  `deps.edn` -- only the central `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry
  itself depends on it, to resolve `:required-technologies` stacks.
- This ADR is EDN-only, matching the live `90-docs/adr` convention
  (commit `6d2d0eeda658`, Remove all .md, normalize ADRs to tx-data)
  -- a single-entity EDN vector with an `:adr/body` string blob
  rather than a paired `.md`+`.edn`.

## Alternatives considered

- Giving the actor an op like `:approve-workshop-clearance` that is
  merely gated behind an always-escalate list: rejected outright per
  the task's explicit domain-design guidance -- an always-escalate op
  still means the *capability* to finalize a workshop-safety-clearance
  decision exists in the code path and is merely deferred to a human
  gate. For a decision this severe (cutting-tool workshop hazard from
  rotary cutters, cutting machines and shears), the capability itself
  must not exist. The only op related to shop-safety escalation is
  `:flag-safety-concern`, which surfaces the need for human/shop-
  safety-officer attention and never itself decides, authorizes, or
  defers a pattern-cutting-execution or workshop-safety-clearance
  decision for later automatic execution.
- Making the supply-cost ceiling a HARD block (mirroring the
  transaction-amount-ceiling HARD check used by some sibling actors):
  rejected per the task's explicit domain design -- an over-threshold
  fabric/pattern-materials order is not itself unsafe, so it is
  handled as a soft escalate gate, not a hard block; only the
  pattern-cutting-execution/workshop-safety-clearance/safety-officer-
  override class is a hard, unconditional block.
- A bare-noun scope-exclusion term list (e.g. cutter, pattern,
  fabric): rejected outright per the fleet-known self-trip bug class
  described above (and CLAUDE.md's own worked example for this exact
  task), and per the explicit instruction to phrase scope-exclusion
  terms as finalization/execution ACTIONS, never bare nouns -- this
  actor's own domain necessarily discusses cutter/pattern/fabric/
  cutting/safety-officer concepts to do its scheduling/logging job,
  so a bare-noun list would risk self-blocking the actor's own
  legitimate proposals (regression-tested directly against rationale
  text that deliberately contains those bare nouns).
- Naming only a single pattern-cutting-execution-finalization op
  family (mirroring a narrower single-category exclusion shape used
  by some sibling actors): rejected -- the task's domain design names
  two distinct excluded decision categories
  (pattern-cutting-execution, workshop-safety-clearance) plus a
  shop-safety-officer-judgment override, so this ADR and the
  governor's `scope-excluded-ops`/`scope-excluded-phrases` name all
  three explicitly rather than conflating them, giving each its own
  dedicated unit test.
- Merging `cutter` and `workshop` into a single entity (only checking
  one provenance record): rejected -- the two-entity provenance shape
  (independently-registered cutter AND independently-registered
  workshop) matches the CRITICAL invariant that a worker/workshop
  record must be independently verified/registered before any action
  and the established pattern from prior `cloud-itonami-isco-*`
  actors with two-sided provenance checks.

## Itemized safety confirmation

Every op in this actor's closed proposal-op allowlist, and why it is
safe under the constraints above:

1. **`:log-work-record`** -- job/pattern/progress data logging only.
   Requires an independently registered cutter AND workshop
   (`:no-cutter`/`:no-workshop`). Not a pattern-cutting-execution or
   workshop-safety-clearance decision in any respect -- it is a
   records-management action.
2. **`:schedule-crew-operation`** -- crew/task scheduling proposal
   only. Cannot carry a pattern-cutting-execution or
   workshop-safety-clearance decision (structurally absent from the
   op's field shape; any such attempt via free text is caught by the
   `:scope-excluded-action` content check). Does not authorize,
   finalize, or draw any conclusion about a specific cut or workshop
   clearance -- it is a calendar/logistics action.
3. **`:flag-safety-concern`** -- surfaces a cutting-tool-hazard/
   material-handling/equipment-condition concern to a human/shop
   safety officer; ALWAYS escalates, unconditionally, at every
   confidence level, NEVER auto-commit eligible. This is the ONLY
   channel through which a safety observation may be surfaced, and it
   explicitly never resolves, decides, or acts on anything itself --
   the shop safety officer reviews and acts entirely on their own
   authority, including any workshop-safety-clearance decision.
4. **`:coordinate-supply-order`** -- fabric/pattern-materials
   procurement proposal only (item, cost). Not a
   pattern-cutting-execution, workshop-safety-clearance or
   authorization decision of any kind -- it is a purchasing-logistics
   action, gated by ordinary cost-ceiling escalation (above 2000
   always escalates).

No op in this allowlist, and no op anywhere in this codebase,
resembles a pattern-cutting-execution decision (e.g. deciding a cut is
complete/final), a workshop-safety-clearance decision (e.g. declaring
the workshop safety-cleared), or a shop-safety-officer-judgment
override -- these categories are structurally absent, not gated,
confirmed by: (a) the closed four-op allowlist itself
(`cutcoord.governor/allowed-ops`), (b) six concretely-named
forbidden-op fixtures rejected at the governor-unit level
(`cutcoord.governor/scope-excluded-ops`,
`cutcoord.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-op-finalize-pattern-cutting-execution-decision`
and four sibling tests), and (c) a content-based scope-exclusion check
independent of the `:op` field
(`cutcoord.governor/scope-excluded-phrases`,
`.../contains-excluded-phrase?`), tested directly against a rationale
that attempts to finalize the pattern-cutting execution, declare the
workshop safety cleared, or override a shop safety officer's judgment
even via an otherwise-allowlisted op
(`cutcoord.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-rationale-even-with-allowlisted-op`,
`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-rationale-declare-workshop-safety-cleared`,
`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-rationale-override-shop-safety-officer`).
")))
